WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.010
Here is a simple, disciplined trading plan.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:08.660
A $100,000 balance. Five trades a day. One percent risk on each.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:14.230
Each position risks $1,000. That is your stop loss.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:19.170
The target is 3 to 5 percent: $3,000 to $5,000 per trade.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.520
And once a trade is in profit, the stop loss moves to break-even.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.380
An example day. Two trades hit take profit: plus $3,000 each.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:34.030
One reaches the profit zone, then returns to break-even: zero.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.380
One hits the stop loss: minus $1,000.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:41.140
And one closes at 5 percent: plus $5,000.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:44.600
The day: plus $10,000. Ten percent.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.990
The edge is the risk-to-reward, not the win rate.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:53.060
At one to three, you break even winning just one trade in four.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:58.090
Even a bad day, one win and four losses, costs only $1,000.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.340
If every trading day matched the example, with risk fixed at $1,000:

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.580
that is $210,000 a month,

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.530
and a balance of $1.36 million after six months.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.850
Now withdraw 3 percent of the balance every month.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.580
By month five, you have taken out more than your initial $100,000.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:26.460
These are projections from one example day, not results.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:36.050
Trading CFDs and FX contracts is highly speculative and involves significant risk to your capital. Never risk more than you can afford.
